%N A401790 Number of permutations of {1..n} such that all primes are in prime positions.
%C A401790 This sequence represents the total number of ways to arrange the elements of the set {1, 2, ..., n} such that every prime number is placed at an index which is also a prime number. Nonprime positions are occupied by nonprime numbers (composite numbers and the number 1).
%C A401790 First differs from A010551 at n=10: a(10) = 17280, while A010551(10) = 14400.
%C A401790 a(489) has 1003 digits. - _Jeff Sponaugle_, Nov 06 2026
%H A401790 Jeff Sponaugle, <a href="/A401790/b401790.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..488</a> (terms 0..100 from Mykola Filipchuk).
%F A401790 a(n) = pi(n)! * (n - pi(n))!, where pi = A000720.
%e A401790 For n = 4, a(4) = 4. The 4 valid permutations are: (1, 2, 3, 4), (1, 3, 2, 4), (4, 2, 3, 1), (4, 3, 2, 1).
%p A401790 a:= n-> (p-> p!*(n-p)!)(numtheory[pi](n)):
%p A401790 seq(a(n), n=0..25);  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Nov 05 2026
%o A401790 (Python)
%o A401790 import math
%o A401790 from sympy import primepi
%o A401790 def a(n):
%o A401790     P = primepi(n)
%o A401790     return math.factorial(P) * math.factorial(n - P)
%o A401790 print([a(n) for n in range(0, 101)])
%o A401790 (PARI) a(n) = my(p=primepi(n)); p!*(n-p)!; \\ _Michel Marcus_, Nov 06 2026
%Y A401790 Cf. A000142, A000720, A010551, A062298.
%K A401790 nonn,easy,new
%O A401790 0,4
